.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1214 PM PDT Mon Sep 7 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

Hazardous conditions at Pacific Coast beaches continues today. Long
period southerly swell from distant Tropical Storm Marie will result
in an increased risk for sneaker waves and strong rip currents with
breaking waves up to 10 feet. Stay off the beach and remain out of
the water to mitigate your risk. Minor coastal flooding will be
possible during high tide this evening for bayshore locations along
the San Pablo Bay in Marin County. The warming and drying trend has
already commenced with conditions notably warmer and drier than they
were 24 hours ago. High pressure will build in from the east
tomorrow, peaking Wednesday and Thursday. Uncertainty in the
forecast stems from Marie`s moisture. Fortunately from a fire
weather standpoint, the mid-level moisture and associated
rain/thunderstorm chances are expected to remain confined to our
south and west. However, associated high-level clouds are expected
to stream in Tuesday into Wednesday which could make highs lower and
lows higher due to decreased solar insolation and radiational
cooling, respectively. We aren`t even going to have the marine layer
to save us this heat event. Reason being, flow aloft has an easterly
component as it wraps into Marie. If we miraculously get any sort of
marine layer, expect it to be compressed below 500 feet which would
bring dense fog to coastal locations. A Heat Advisory has been
issued for all areas and will run from 10AM Wednesday through 10PM
Thursday. Temperatures will be 10 to 20 degrees above normal with
minimal onshore flow. There are high probabilities for moderate and
even major HeatRisk during this time; in fact, San Francisco has a
high probability (70%+) that the eastern half of the city will reach
or exceed a maximum temperature of 80 degrees on Wednesday. Onshore
flow is expected to return on Thursday which may be enough to remove
coastal locations from the Heat Advisory; however, we will likely
wait to see how Wednesday`s forecast fares before making any
changes. To put this heat event into context, 850 mb temperatures
and 500 mb heights are going to be between the 90th percentile and
daily record values which indicates that we are dealing with an
anomalously warm airmass with higher than normal heights. Two things
that are preventing this from being a widespread record breaking
event is September is climatologically the warmest month of the year
for most of our area and we have benchmark records dating back to
1944.

